WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.140
Hydrogen rich gas accumulates on a neutron star.

00:00:03.220 --> 00:00:06.680
In this lecture’s thermonuclear burst model, hydrogen burning

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:09.540
builds a helium layer. When helium ignites rapidly,

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:12.320
the surface flares and then settles. The neutron

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:15.500
star survives and can accumulate more fuel. A

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:18.120
rotating pulsar beam is a different explanation for

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:19.580
regularly repeating light.

00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:23.270
A white dwarf can also receive gas, but

00:00:23.270 --> 00:00:26.490
the classical nova ignites hydrogen in its accreted

00:00:26.490 --> 00:00:30.110
surface layer. Expanding material makes the system brighten.

00:00:30.250 --> 00:00:33.470
The underlying white dwarf survives, so later accretion

00:00:33.470 --> 00:00:36.010
can replenish the fuel. This differs from an

00:00:36.010 --> 00:00:38.410
explosion that disrupts the whole white dwarf.

00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:42.220
Compare the accretor, the explosive fuel and the

00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:44.880
aftermath. Helium on a neutron star gives the

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:47.340
burst model; hydrogen on a white dwarf gives

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:49.700
a classical nova. The full Type One A

00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:52.880
model disrupts a carbon and oxygen white dwarf.

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:56.820
No compact stellar remnant still allows expanding ejecta

00:00:56.820 --> 00:00:58.620
and an extended remnant of gas.
